
* add flag for "don't make allowances for non-hashbang calls"
   - right now, if the interpreter flags are pre-split, it is handled
* change c code to c++
   - syntax is mostly there; the remaining C is malloc/calloc/char** arrays
   - replace argv_t with vector<string>, build char*[] only for execvp
     (the fixed MAX_STR_CONST buffers caused heap overflows, now patched)
* optional library helper that reads the script's own #! line for parse_switches()
  (read_hashbang() does most of it)
* split_string() quirks found by t/splitcompare.pl: DECIDED to leave as is (documented in the
  man page, QUOTING AND ESCAPES and BUGS); the default splitter (shellwords) avoids them.
  Revisit only if one of them bites:
   - -s keeps a backslash before whitespace or a quote (t/ks*/exp3 currently expects this)
   - -p: empty quotes directly followed by text give an extra empty arg
   - a quote with nothing after it is not reported as unmatched
   - -s also strips backslashes inside single quotes (the shell and shellwords keep them)
* remove the legacy splitter and the -L/-s/-p/-x implication eventually (3.0?)
* IDEA: optional ${VAR} expansion of environment variables in the #! line.  GNU env -S has it
  (only the ${VARNAME} form; an unset variable gives an empty string; no expansion inside single
  quotes; a bare $VAR is an error).  Design: an opt-in flag (e.g. -E), expand AFTER splitting so a
  value with spaces stays one argument, and decide whether variables set by ~/.env2rc are visible
  (vars2 currently runs after the #! line is split).
* use namespace cwputils
* break env2() into phases (parse #!, find script, merge config args, emit)
   - escr_loc is not reliably set; track the script index explicitly
* put the duplicated escape table (array_utils.cc, vars2.cc) and #defines in one header
* add -Wextra to the configure-generated CXXFLAGS and clean up the warnings
* fixture dirs t/ks0, t/ks1 are mostly duplicates
* unit-test split_string() directly

DONE
* version 2.0: split_shellwords(), a port of Perl's shellwords() verified against Perl on all strings
  up to length 6 (make test), is the DEFAULT splitter.  The original splitter is "legacy": -L selects
  it, and -s, -p, -x imply it (so older #! lines are unchanged).  -S is still accepted.
* test_env2.sh runs with a private HOME (it used to leave a copy of t/env2rc in the real ~/.env2rc)
* parse_switches(): table-driven, Perl-style switch parser in the library; env2 uses it;
  sample_interpreter3 shows custom use (replaces the old copy-of-env2 parser, 2020)
* help text is generated from the switch table (switch_help()), so it can't drift from the parser
* test_configure.sh: configure + env2 tested against linux/naive/perfect kernels via t/fakekernel.pl
* KERNEL_SPLIT 2 folded into 1 (no such kernel known; t/ks2 was a copy of t/ks1)
* flags is a plain struct Flags passed by const reference (no global, no init_flags)
* -w flag (Cygwin script path to Windows format)
* -v flag prints the version
* make distclean can remove config.mk; header deps via -MMD (no more
  Makefile rule that deleted the .cc files)
* heap overflows in split_string()/env2(), crash on #! line with no args
